    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery right now and I'm running into a frustrating issue. Every time I try to cut the dovetails, the wood fibers are splintering at the tips of the pins, even though I'm being careful with the layout. It's compromising the fit and making the joints look messy. Can you give me some advice on how to manage the grain direction better or what I should check with my chisel sharpness?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This often happens when the chisel is slightly dull or the wood grain is tight. Ensure your chisel is razor-sharp, as a slight burr will tear the fibers instead of slicing them cleanly. Also, try to maintain a very shallow, controlled angle when paring your waste material to guide the cut along the grain rather than against it.",
      "source": "target",
